already set, super().format uses it; fine.\n\nThen multiline: `if \"\\n\" in msg and rec.message:` — rec.message is set by super().format (on the copy) to rec.getMessage(). Good, prefix computed from msg.find(rec.message). If the truncated message occurs in prefix? Prefix is \"[LEVEL date file:line] \" which doesn't contain message typically. Fine (original code same).\n\nWait, but if record.message in original was empty... same as before.\n\nTruncation function:\n\n```python\n_OMITTED_MARKER = \"...<omitted>...\"\n\ndef _truncate_message(message, budget):\n    marker = _OMITTED_MARKER\n    remaining = budget - len(marker)\n    head = (remaining + 1) // 2\n    tail = remaining - head\n    return message[:head] + marker + (message[-tail:] if tail else \"\")\n```\n\nExample budget 32: marker len 15, remaining 17, head=9, tail=8. ✓ message[-0:] edge handled.\n\nDefault handler: `_setup_logger` reads envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS. Import from deepinfer.environ — check environ doesn't import logger (circular). environ imports only os, threading etc. Fine.\n\nBut environ.py imports? logger importing environ: environ imports nothing from logger. But environ.py maybe imports other modules? From shown head, only os/threading/collections/datetime/typing. Good.\n\nHowever importing `envs` at module import time in logger.py — deepinfer/environ.py may be heavy? Fine.\n\nWhere to import: at top `from deepinfer.environ import envs`. But maybe safer lazy import inside _setup_logger to avoid circular import chains (some modules import logger then environ reads envs... independent). Actually EnvVar resolution in _setup_logger occurs at module import time (since _setup_logger() called at import). If env var invalid, import fails. Tests may expect ValueError when initializing logger with invalid env? Test `test_invalid_budget` probably constructs formatter directly, and default init tests may set env invalid and call _setup_logger expecting ValueError. Let's just do it.\n\nBette